\textbf{\textcolor{red}{FOC for maximization --- HIGH SCHOOL (free max) vs UNIVERSITY (KKT, constrained).}}

\begin{tabular}{p{17.2cm}|p{17.2cm}}
\textbf{\textcolor{red}{HS version: free max (F)}} & \textbf{\textcolor{red}{Uni version: constrained max (P), KKT}} \\
\textit{Problem (F):} $\max_{x \in \mathbb{R}^N} f(x)$. & \textit{Problem (P):} $\max_x f(x)$ \textcolor{red}{\;s.t.\; $g_k(x) \geq 0,\;k=1,\ldots,K$.}  \\
\hline
\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Necessity ($\Rightarrow$).}} \underline{If} $x^*$ is a local max, \textcolor{blue}{$f$ differentiable}, \underline{then}
\[ \boxed{\;\nabla f(x^*) = 0.\;} \]
\emph{\textcolor{orange}{$\nabla f(x^*) = 0$ is a necessary condition for a max.}}
&
\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Necessity ($\Rightarrow$).}} \underline{If} \textcolor{red}{$x^*$ is feasible ($g_k(x^*) \geq 0\;\forall k$)} and a local max, \textcolor{blue}{$f, g_k$ differentiable} and \textcolor{red}{CQ} holds at $x^*$, \underline{then} \textbf{KKT conditions hold}: $\exists$ nonnegative scalars $\lambda^* = [\lambda_1^*, \ldots, \lambda_K^*]$ such that
\[ \tfrac{\partial \mathcal{L}(x^*, \lambda^*)}{\partial x_n} = \tfrac{\partial f(x^*)}{\partial x_n} + \textcolor{red}{\sum_k \lambda_k^*\, \tfrac{\partial g_k(x^*)}{\partial x_n}} = 0, \]
plus \textbf{\textcolor{red}{CS}}: \textcolor{red}{$\lambda_k^*\, g_k(x^*) = 0\;\forall k$}, that is $\forall k$ if $g_k(x^*) > 0 \rightarrow  \lambda_k^* = 0$.
\emph{\textcolor{orange}{KKT are necessary conditions for a max.}}
Note here we have a system with n + k unknowns! If you solve it you find the $x*$ and $k$ that make the lagrangian equal to 0 .
\\
\hline
\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Sufficiency ($\Leftarrow$).}} \underline{If} \textcolor{blue}{$f$ differentiable and concave} \underline{then} $\nabla f(x^*) = 0$ \emph{iff} $x^*$ solves (F).
\emph{\textcolor{orange}{Under concavity, FOC is also sufficient.}}
&
\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Sufficiency ($\Leftarrow$).}} \underline{If} \textcolor{blue}{$f, g_k$ concave and differentiable} and \textcolor{red}{$\exists\,\underline{x}$ such that $g_k(\underline{x}) > 0\;\forall k$} (\textcolor{blue}{Slater}), \underline{then} \textcolor{red}{a feasible $x^*$ solves (P) \emph{iff} KKT holds.}
\emph{\textcolor{orange}{Under concavity + Slater, KKT is necessary \emph{and} sufficient (iff).}}
\\
\hline
\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Notes.}}
$\bullet$ Without \textcolor{blue}{concavity}, $\nabla f(x^*) = 0$ alone $\Rightarrow$ $x^*$ is only a \emph{stationary point} (min, saddle, or max).
$\bullet$ Nonneg-constrained variant ($x_n \geq 0$), \emph{necessity only}: $\;\tfrac{\partial f(x^*)}{\partial x_n} \leq 0,\;\; = 0\text{ if } x_n^* > 0.$

{{img:0|w=800}}
&
\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Notes.}}
$\bullet$ Without \textcolor{blue}{concavity} + Slater, KKT only implies $x^*$ is \emph{stationary} (could be local max, min, or saddle).
\\
\end{tabular}

Recall that $\lambda$ is the marginal value of relaxing that constraint! and recall also that if Inada and satiation cannot help you you need to verify is a constraint is slack, either a priori or ex post. 


\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Key implication of the sufficiency side.}}

\underline{If} the \emph{sufficient}-side assumptions hold ---  \textcolor{blue}{$f, g_k$ concave and differentiable} and \textcolor{blue}{Slater} (\textcolor{red}{$\exists\,\underline{x}$ such that $g_k(\underline{x}) > 0\;\forall k$}) --- \underline{then}:
\[ \boxed{\;x^* \text{ satisfies KKT} \;\;\Longrightarrow\;\; x^* \text{ is a feasible (global) maximum of (P).}\;} \]

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Practical recipe.}} For problem (P): $\max_{x \in \mathbb{R}^N} f(x)$ s.t.\ $g(x) \geq 0$ (i.e.\ $g_k(x) \geq 0,\;k=1,\ldots,K$):
\begin{enumerate}
\item Write all constraints in \textcolor{orange}{canonical form} $g(x) \geq 0$.
\item Write the Lagrangian: $\mathcal{L}(x, \lambda) = f(x) + \lambda \cdot g(x)$.
\item Recall $\lambda^* \geq 0$.
\item \textbf{FOCs --- one per component} $x_n$, $n = 1, \ldots, N$:\[ \tfrac{\partial \mathcal{L}(x^*, \lambda^*)}{\partial x_n} \;=\; \tfrac{\partial f(x^*)}{\partial x_n} + \sum_{k=1}^{K} \lambda_k^*\, \tfrac{\partial g_k(x^*)}{\partial x_n} \;=\; 0. \]\emph{$N$ equations, one for each decision variable $x_n$.} BUT REMEMBER YOUR LAGRANGIAN IN A N + K VARIABLES SYSTEM! 

\item \textcolor{red}{Complementary slackness (CS): $\lambda_k^*\, g_k(x^*) = 0\;\forall k$.} IF THE COSTRAINT BINDS, YOU CAN USE IT AS AN EQUATION INT HE SYSTEM WITH EQUALITY. THIS IS THE ROLE THE BUDGET CONSTRAINT HAS. SO BASICLALY UOUU MERGE THE FOCS AND YOU SUSBITTUE THEM IN THE BC EQUATION.

\item Verify feasibility ex post: $g(\hat x) \geq 0$ for non-binding constraints, $\lambda_{\hat k} \geq 0$ for assumed-binding ones. If any check fails, re-guess which constraints bind.  
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{How to read the output.}}
$\bullet$ If \textcolor{blue}{concavity + Slater hold} $\Rightarrow$ steps 1--6 give the \emph{full} characterization of the optimum (\textcolor{orange}{necessary \emph{and} sufficient}).
$\bullet$ If only \textcolor{blue}{differentiability + CQ} hold (no concavity) $\Rightarrow$ steps 1--6 are only \textcolor{orange}{necessary}; the candidate $x^*$ may be a local max, min, or saddle. Must compare candidates or use second-order conditions.


\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Appendix --- Constraint Qualifications (CQ).}}

\emph{\textcolor{orange}{Constraint qualifications ensure that the active constraints describe the local shape of the feasible region accurately using first-order (gradient) information.}}

KKT describes the feasible region through a \textbf{first-order linear approximation} of each active constraint $g_k$ at $x^*$: it uses only the gradient $\nabla g_k(x^*)$ and asks in which directions the linearised constraint would allow a step. When this linear picture is faithful to the true (curved) feasible region, KKT is a valid necessary condition for optimality. When it isn't --- because the linear approximation misses the shape of the constraint --- KKT can fail even at a genuine optimum. A CQ is precisely the condition that rules out this pathology.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Two constraints, same feasible point $x^* = 0$, opposite verdicts.}}

\textbf{Constraint A.}\quad $g(x) = -x$, so the constraint $g(x) \geq 0$ reads $x \leq 0$. Gradient at $0$: $g'(0) = -1 \neq 0$.
$\bullet$ Move left ($x < 0$): $g(x) > 0$ --- \emph{feasible}.
$\bullet$ Move right ($x > 0$): $g(x) < 0$ --- \emph{infeasible}.
The gradient tells you \emph{exactly} which side is allowed. The linear picture matches the truth. \textbf{\textcolor{red}{CQ holds.}}

\textbf{Constraint B.}\quad $g(x) = -x^2$, so the constraint $g(x) \geq 0$ reads $x^2 \leq 0$. Gradient at $0$: $g'(0) = 0$.
$\bullet$ Linear picture: $g'(0) \cdot d = 0 \geq 0$ for \emph{any} direction $d$, so KKT thinks every step is allowed.
$\bullet$ Truth: $(-0.001)^2 > 0$ and $(0.001)^2 > 0$ --- \emph{no} step is feasible. The only feasible point is $x = 0$ itself.
The linear picture lies. \textbf{\textcolor{red}{CQ fails.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Where does the failure come from?}}

KKT captures only the \textbf{linear} component of the Taylor expansion of $g_k$ around $x^*$ (the effect of a small step $d$ through $g_k(x^*+d) \approx g_k(x^*) + \nabla g_k(x^*)^\top d$). The \textbf{non-linear} (curvature) component is invisible to the KKT conditions. When curvature dominates near $x^*$ --- as in Constraint B, where $g''(0) = -2$ carries all the local information --- the linear picture is uninformative and KKT can fail even at a genuine optimum. Constraint qualifications (\textcolor{blue}{LICQ}, \textcolor{blue}{MFCQ}, \textcolor{blue}{Slater}, \ldots) are exactly the conditions that require the gradients of the \emph{active} constraints to carry enough first-order information to describe the local feasible cone.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{REPRESENTATIVE AGENT ASSUMPTION --- when can a single agent stand in for the whole population?}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Definition.}}
A \emph{representative agent} is a single hypothetical consumer whose demand reproduces aggregate demand. Formally: there exists a utility function $U^R$ and a wealth $W$ such that
\[ \boxed{\;\sum_h x_i^*(p, w^h; U^h) \;=\; x_i^*(p, W; U^R) \quad \forall\, i.\;} \]
\emph{When this works, macro/aggregative analysis collapses to a single optimization problem --- a huge workhorse simplification.}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{When is the assumption valid?}}
Aggregate demand must depend on \emph{total} wealth only, \textbf{not on its distribution} across agents:
\[ \sum_h x_i^*(p, w^h) \;=\; X_i(p, W), \quad W = \textstyle\sum_h w^h. \]

\textbf{Two canonical families that satisfy Gorman aggregation:}

\bigskip

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{1. Homothetic preferences} --- whatever the wealth, agents all consume the same RATIO.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{2. Quasilinear preferences} --- whatever the endowment, agents all consume the same NON-NUMERAIRE $y$.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{2.6 --- INDIRECT UTILITY $v(p,w)$ \& ROY'S IDENTITY.}}

\begin{keydef}
\textbf{Proposition (Roy's identity).} If $x^*$ satisfies Walras's law and $v$ is differentiable:
\[ x_n^*(p, w) = -\dfrac{\partial v(p, w)/\partial p_n}{\partial v(p, w)/\partial w}, \qquad n = 1, \ldots, N. \]
\end{keydef}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{CONTRACT CURVE --- the subset of PS that's mutually-improving over $\omega$.}}

\emph{Plain English.} The Contract Curve is the set of allocations that are both \textbf{Pareto efficient} AND make \textbf{both agents at least as well off as at their endowment $\omega$}.  These are the allocations a pair of agents would ``sign a contract'' for: no party walks away worse off than autarky ($\omega$), and there's no further mutual gain left to extract.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{(1) Definition.}}
\[ \boxed{\;\mathrm{CC}(\omega) \;=\; \bigl\{x \in \mathrm{PS} \;:\; U^i(x^i) \,\geq\, U^i(\omega^i) \;\;\forall i\bigr\}.\;} \]
Two ingredients:
$\bullet$ \emph{Efficiency}: $x \in \mathrm{PS}$ (cannot improve everyone).
$\bullet$ \emph{Individual rationality}: each agent weakly prefers $x$ to $\omega$ (voluntary participation).


{{img:0|w=900}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{PARETO OPTIMUM --- UPF and PS, the two coordinate views.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{(1) UPF --- where it lives.}}
$\mathrm{UPF} \subset \mathcal{U}$, where
\[ \mathcal{U} \;=\; \bigl\{(u^1, u^2) \;:\; \exists\, x \in \mathcal{F},\; U^i(x^i) \geq u^i \;\forall i\bigr\} \]
is the set of \emph{utility profiles dominated by some feasible allocation}.

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{(2) UPF --- definition (no domination).}}
$(\bar u^1, \bar u^2) \in \mathcal{U}$ is on the UPF iff
\[ \boxed{\;\nexists\, (\tilde u^1, \tilde u^2) \in \mathcal{U} : \tilde u^i \geq \bar u^i \;\forall i,\; > \text{some } j.\;} \]
set of utiliteis that are undominated

{{img:0|w=580}}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{(4) PS --- definition (no domination).}}
$\bar x \in \mathcal{F}$ is Pareto Optimal iff
\[ \boxed{\;\nexists\, y \in \mathcal{F} : U^i(y^i) \geq U^i(\bar x^i) \;\forall i,\; > \text{some } j.\;} \]
and $\mathrm{PS} = \{x \in \mathcal{F} : x \text{ is PO}\}$. Basically is an allocaiton taht cannot e dominated.

\textcolor{orange}{\emph{The allocation-space picture (Edgeworth box) lives in its own snippet --- ``PS in the Edgeworth box''.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{(5) Finding PS $\equiv$ finding UPF.}}
The two problems are equivalent via the utility map: every $x^* \in \mathrm{PS}$ pushes forward to a UPF point, and every UPF point is the image of some $x^* \in \mathrm{PS}$. Formally:
\[ \boxed{\;\mathrm{PS} \;\subset\; \mathcal{F} \;\;\xrightarrow{\;(U^1,\, U^2)\;}\;\; \mathrm{UPF} \;\subset\; \mathcal{U}.\;} \]

\textcolor{orange}{\emph{Comment: the UPF is the set of all PO allocations viewed in utility coordinates instead of allocation coordinates.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{PS in the Edgeworth box --- allocation-space view of the UPF.}}

This snippet is the \emph{allocation-space} picture of the very same Pareto-efficient set that appears as the UPF in $(u^1, u^2)$ space. The link is the utility map:
\[ \boxed{\;\text{PS}\;\subset\;\mathcal{F}\;\;\xrightarrow{\;(U^1,\,U^2)\;}\;\;\text{UPF}\;\subset\;\mathcal{U}.\;} \]
\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Every gold PO point shown below sits on the UPF}} of the companion snippet --- same set, two coordinate systems.

\textbf{\textcolor{darkgreen}{Picture --- Edgeworth box.}} The green curve is the \textbf{Pareto Set} (PS): the locus of all Pareto-efficient allocations, independent of $\omega$. 

{{img:0|w=900}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Reading the link to the UPF.}}
$\bullet$ \emph{Each gold PO point} in this Edgeworth box is sent by $(U^1, U^2)$ to a point on the UPF (other snippet).
$\bullet$ \emph{The whole green PS curve} maps onto the whole UPF curve --- nothing is missed.
$\bullet$ \emph{The supporting price slope} $-p_1/p_2$ here corresponds to the isowelfare slope $-\lambda/(1-\lambda)$ on the UPF.

\textcolor{orange}{\emph{Important: Contract Curve vs Pareto Set.} The Contract Curve is \emph{not} the green curve --- it is the subset of PS that Pareto-dominates a given endowment $\omega$ (the agents agree to trade because both end up at least as well off as at $\omega$). PS is endowment-free; the Contract Curve depends on $\omega$ and is only a piece of PS.}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Cross-references.}}
$\bullet$ \emph{Pareto Optimum (PS \& UPF)} --- definitions, the duality box, two planner characterizations.
$\bullet$ \emph{Contract Curve} --- the endowment-dependent subset of PS.
$\bullet$ \emph{Exchange Economy} --- defines $\mathcal{F}$.

{{img:0|w=900}}

\emph{Reading the Edgeworth picture.} Endowment $\omega = (8, 2)$ for agent 1, $(2, 6)$ for agent 2. Budget line through $\omega$ with slope $-\hat p_a/\hat p_b$. The CE allocation is the unique point where \emph{both} agents' ICs are tangent to the budget line (MRS$^1$ = MRS$^2$ = $\hat p_a/\hat p_b$) --- hence on the contract curve.


\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Four observations on the Edgeworth picture (notes IMG\_1659).}}

\textbf{1) Where the indifference curves sit at $\omega$.}
At the endowment $\omega$, agent 1's IC and agent 2's IC pass through the same point but generally are \emph{not} tangent there. The CE is where, after the rotation of the BC, both ICs become tangent to the BC line --- that tangency point is on the contract curve.

\textbf{2) The BC line is just $[\mathrm{A}]$ rewritten.}
Solve the consumer's budget identity for $x^1_\ell$:
\[ \boxed{\;x^1_\ell \;=\; \omega^1_\ell \;+\; \omega^1_h\,\frac{p_h}{p_\ell} \;-\; \frac{p_h}{p_\ell}\,x^1_h.\;} \]
A change in the price ratio $p_h/p_\ell$ \emph{rotates} the BC about $\omega$: the slope of the BC line changes but it still passes through $\omega$. Different rotations $=$ different sets of \emph{feasible} (affordable) bundles for the agent.

\textbf{3) Edges of the box $\Leftrightarrow$ corner solutions.}
An agent ends up on the edge of the box exactly when he sells his \emph{full endowment} of one good. At that allocation:
\[ x^i_n = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \text{the marginal utility of good } n \text{ for agent } i \text{ is } 0\; (\text{at the corner)}. \]
(With strictly monotone $+$ strictly concave preferences, this won't happen in equilibrium; agents stay in the interior. But it's a real possibility once any of these assumptions break.)

\textbf{4) Feasibility}
$\bullet$ \emph{Feasibility}: all points in the Edgeworth box are feasible (no resource is wasted; total endowment is conserved).

\textbf{5) CE and PO}
- You solve the individual maximizaiton and you get the individual optimum where each individual indifference curve touches the budget set
- IF market clears, it must be because individual optimum touches, so you see the two indiff cures touching hte budget constraint in the same point. Note wihtout strict monotonicity, strict convexity of preferences and continuity the CE may not exist. Meaning, the agents are not meeting on the same point. 
- Characterization: the PO is where the indifference curves are tangent. SO if we have a ce it is a po. But we may have some PO that are not supportable by any system of prices. Is not that you cannto fit a tangent, is that the agents do not want to stay on the poitn where the liens touch, they want to go to the corner

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{CONDITIONS FOR EXISTENCE OF CE.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{(1) Standard sufficient conditions.}}
$\bullet$ \textcolor{blue}{Continuity} of preferences.
$\bullet$ \textcolor{blue}{Strict monotonicity} (consumers want more --- LNS).
$\bullet$ \textcolor{blue}{Strict convexity} (consumers prefer averages; concave $U^i$).

Under (continuity + monotonicity + convexity) a CE exists at any $\omega$ (Arrow--Debreu). Break \emph{any one} of the three and CE may fail. The three failure modes, one per broken assumption:

\begin{tabular}{p{9.5cm}|p{9.5cm}|p{9.5cm}|p{9.5cm}}
\textbf{\textcolor{darkgreen}{FAILURE 1 --- Non-monotonicity (satiation).}} \emph{Broken: monotonicity.} & \textbf{\textcolor{darkgreen}{FAILURE 2 --- Non-convexity (no supporting price).}} \emph{Broken: convexity.} & \textbf{\textcolor{darkgreen}{FAILURE 3 --- Discontinuous demand.}} \emph{Broken: continuity.} & \textbf{\textcolor{darkgreen}{FAILURE 3 (concrete) --- Leontief vs. max.}} \emph{Same broken: continuity.} \\
\hline
\emph{Setup.} Agent 2 gets utility from \textbf{only one} of the two goods --- say good $h$. Endowment $\omega^2 = (0, \omega^2_h)$ --- agent 2 starts with all of good $h$, none of good $\ell$. Notice for agent 2 the more to the bottom the red line the better. Notice the PO set exists and is wherever we give 0 of good l to agent 2. But here the CE does not exist because agents maxima point at different loci.

\textbf{What goes wrong (two cases):}
$\bullet$ \emph{Case 1: $p_h = 0$.} Agent 1 (monotonic) demands infinite $x_h$, finite supply $\Rightarrow$ excess demand. \textbf{No clearing.} When price is 0 it is always like this.
$\bullet$ \emph{Case 2: $p_h > 0$, $p_\ell > 0$.}
- Agent 2 doesn't value $x_\ell$, is optimum is always on the edge of th ebox.
- Agent 1 (with normal preferences). Woual always optimize ina  point in the center of the box (in this case A). But agent 2 won't part with any $x_h$ at any positive price. \textbf{No counterparty $\Rightarrow$ agent 1 stuck at $\omega^1$, not at his optimum.}
$\rightarrow$ \textbf{Agent 2 has no incentive to trade --- he stays exactly at $\omega^2$}. \emph{Result}: no price clears with both agents optimizing. \textbf{No CE.} Note instead how the PO points exist!

