\textbf{\textcolor{red}{MODELLING TAXATION (HSV FRAMEWORK).}}

Gov spends each year  G (defense, healthcare...)
\[ G = \int T(y)\, df(y) \;\;\;\;\textbf{[A]} \quad\quad\text{Totally diff than before.} \]\[ T(y) = y - c, \quad \text{where} \;\; \boxed{\;c \;=\; B\, y^{1-\tau},\;\; B > 0,\;\; \tau \in [0, 1].\;} \]
\emph{So planner chooses $B, \tau$ to satisfy [A]. Note: if you choose $\tau$, $B$ needs to adjust to have G.}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Reading $\tau$ (progressivity dial).}}
$\bullet$ \emph{If $\tau = 0$} (0 progressivity), tax is indep.\ of income: $c = B y$ (flat tax, \% of $y$).
$\bullet$ \emph{If $\tau > 0$}, $c < y$ $\to$ \textbf{progressive tax} (rich pay an higher %) 
$\bullet$ \emph{$\tau = 1$}: total tax, $c = B$, \emph{we all consume the same amount} (communism). Hyper progressivity: the rich pays so much he gets to the poor level of consumption.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Public goods --- planner's problem and Samuelson condition.}}

\emph{Setup from preamble snippet: $U^i(x^i, y)$, both agents consume the SAME $y$. Input $z = c(y)$ of private good produces $y$.}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Planner's problem.}}
\[ \max_{x^1, x^2, y, z}\;\; U^1(x^1, y) + U^2(x^2, y) \quad \text{sub.}\;\;\;\; \underbrace{x^1 + x^2}_{\text{private cons.}} + \underbrace{z}_{\text{input devoted to }y} \;\le\; \omega^1 + \omega^2 \quad (\mu), \quad \underbrace{c(y)}_{\text{cost of output}} \;\le\; \underbrace{z}_{\text{input devoted}} \quad (\varphi). \]

SEE HOW IT IS THE SAME Y!!!!!!!!!!!

\emph{Both constraints bind at the optimum (idle input is wasted utility).}


\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Lagrangian.}}
\[ \mathcal{L} = U^1(x^1, y) + U^2(x^2, y) - \mu(x^1 + x^2 + z - \omega^1 - \omega^2) - \varphi(c(y) - z). \]

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{FOCs.}}
\[ \boxed{\dfrac{\partial U^1}{\partial x^1} = \mu}, \;\;\; \boxed{\dfrac{\partial U^2}{\partial x^2} = \mu}, \;\;\; \boxed{\varphi = \mu}, \;\;\; \boxed{\dfrac{\partial U^1}{\partial y} + \dfrac{\partial U^2}{\partial y} = \mu\, c'(y)}. \]

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Samuelson condition} (the big FOC).} Divide the last FOC by $\mu = \partial U^i/\partial x^i$:
\[ \boxed{\;\; \underbrace{\dfrac{\partial U^1/\partial y}{\partial U^1/\partial x^1}}_{MRS^1_{y,x}} + \underbrace{\dfrac{\partial U^2/\partial y}{\partial U^2/\partial x^2}}_{MRS^2_{y,x}} \;=\; \underbrace{c'(y)}_{MRT_{y,x}} \;\;} \]
note in quasilienar case with mu = 1 you would have had as samuelson condition the 4th foc. 
note this is just 4 with mu on the left hansd side. note that 4 is basicaly saying: at the margin the amrgical cost of transforming one x in y is $c'(y)$  (MRT) times the lpportunity cost of x that had a value of mu  (**you give up 5 x to produce 1 y, and these 5 x are worth 2 so the utility cost is 10!**)! so when I consume y I lose twice: i lose the value of the x i iput and the fact the technology is unfavourable. 

\textcolor{purple}{\emph{Reading.}} The planner equates the \textbf{marginal cost of production} $c'(y)$ to the \textbf{social marginal benefit of production} = sum of agents' marginal benefits (MRS). The standard private-good condition ``MRS = MRT'' generalises to ``$\sum MRS = MRT$'' for a public good --- because each unit of $y$ is enjoyed by ALL agents simultaneously, the social MB is the SUM of individual MBs.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Public goods --- three classical solutions to under-provision.}}

\emph{From the CE snippet: each contributor sets $MRS^i = p_y/p_x$, but Samuelson requires $\sum_i MRS^i = p_y/p_x = MRT$. Gap: $y^{CE} < y^{*\,Planner}$. Three classical fixes restore Pareto-optimality.}

\textbf{\textcolor{darkgreen}{A. Quota (direct mandate).}} Government mandates each agent's contribution $\bar y^i$ such that $\sum_i \bar y^i = y^*$ (the Samuelson level). Each agent has no choice: $y^i = \bar y^i$, the rest of his income goes to $x^i$.

\emph{Issues:} (1) planner must know $y^*$ and the right distribution of contributions; (2) enforcement / compliance; (3) blunt: doesn't use price signals at all.

\textbf{\textcolor{darkgreen}{B. Public provision via lump-sum tax.}} Government directly produces $y^*$ at cost $p_x\, c(y^*)$ and finances it with lump-sum taxes $T^i$ on agents, with $\sum_i T^i = p_x\, c(y^*)$ (balanced budget).

\emph{Each agent's budget:} $p_x x^i = p_x \omega^i + \theta^i \pi^* - T^i$ (no choice on $y$, just on $x^i$).


\textbf{\textcolor{darkgreen}{C. Bilateral subsidy (Pigou-like for positive externalities).}}

\emph{Idea:} when agent $i$ contributes a unit of $y^i$, total $\hat y$ rises by 1, which benefits every OTHER agent. This positive externality is exactly the missing piece in CE. \textbf{Subsidise each agent's contribution by the marginal external benefit he creates for the others.}

\emph{Subsidy rate for agent $i$ (per unit of $y^i$, in \$):}
\[ s^i \;=\; \sum_{j \ne i} \dfrac{\partial U^j/\partial y}{\partial U^j/\partial x^j}\bigg|_{\hat y} \cdot p_x \;=\; p_x\, \sum_{j \ne i} MRS^j\Big|_{\hat y}. \]

in the QL case with alfa = 1 we have no demoniator and px = 1. the idea above is that we need to translate utility in dollars, as we are givign to the other guy money. If you use just the theominator you have: give him a subsidy equal to utility units. If you divide by the numeraire you say: 3 units of standardized utility * the cost of one unit of standardize dutilty. Easist case: numeraire is the dollar, so 3 units of standardied utilty * 1 dollar. 

\emph{Agent $i$'s budget with subsidy:}
\[ p_x x^i + (p_y - s^i)\, y^i \;=\; p_x \omega^i + \theta^i \pi^*. \]

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{FOC:}}
\[ \boxed{\;\dfrac{\partial U^i}{\partial y}\Big|_{\hat y} = \dfrac{p_y - s^i}{p_x}\, \dfrac{\partial U^i}{\partial x^i}\;\; \Longleftrightarrow \;\; MRS^i\Big|_{\hat y} = \dfrac{p_y}{p_x} - \sum_{j\ne i} MRS^j\Big|_{\hat y}\;} \]
Rearranging:
\[ \boxed{\;\; \sum_k MRS^k\Big|_{\hat y} \;=\; \dfrac{p_y}{p_x} \;=\; c'(y) \;=\; MRT \;\;} \]
\textbf{This is exactly the Samuelson condition.} The bilateral subsidy makes each agent internalise the spillover his contribution creates on the others. Output matches the planner's.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Two-agent worked example.}} With $U^i = \alpha_i \ln y + x^i$, $c(y) = \tfrac{1}{2} y^2$, and $p_x = p_y = 1$:

\emph{Planner:} $\sum_i \alpha_i/y = y \;\Rightarrow\; y^* = \sqrt{\alpha_1 + \alpha_2}$.

\emph{Pre-policy CE:} only the higher-$\alpha$ agent (say agent 1) contributes; $y^{CE} = \sqrt{\alpha_1} < y^*$.

\emph{Bilateral subsidy:} $s^1 = MRS^2|_{\hat y} = \alpha_2/\hat y$, $s^2 = \alpha_1/\hat y$. Agent $i$'s FOC: $\alpha_i/\hat y = 1 - s^i = 1 - \alpha_{-i}/\hat y$. Summing: $(\alpha_1 + \alpha_2)/\hat y = 2 - (\alpha_1 + \alpha_2)/\hat y \Rightarrow \hat y = \sqrt{\alpha_1 + \alpha_2} = y^*$. \textbf{Allocation matches the planner.}

\textbf{\textcolor{darkgreen}{D. Tax-based equivalents (when you prefer a tax to a subsidy).}} The bilateral subsidy in C can be replaced by a TAX that delivers the same marginal incentive. Three equivalent tax instruments:

\emph{D.1 Tax on private good $x^i$.} Raise the effective price of $x$ by $t$:
\[ p_x x^i + p_y y^i = M^i \quad \to \quad (p_x + t)\, x^i + p_y\, y^i = M^i. \]
FOC: $MRS^i = p_y/(p_x + t)$. Raising $t$ lowers $MRS^i$, hence raises $y^{i*}$. \emph{Achieves same allocation as subsidy on $y$; relative price moved on the $x$ side rather than the $y$ side.}

\emph{D.2 Shortfall tax} on under-contribution: tax each agent $t$ per unit by which $y^i$ falls short of the planner target $y^*$:
\[ \text{tax payment} \;=\; t\,(y^* - y^i). \]
Agent $i$'s budget:
\[ p_x x^i + p_y y^i = M^i - t\,(y^* - y^i) \;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\; p_x x^i + (p_y - t)\, y^i = \underbrace{M^i - t y^*}_{\text{lump-sum hit}}. \]
FOC: $MRS^i = (p_y - t)/p_x$. \textbf{Identical FOC to bilateral subsidy with $s = t$.}

\emph{D.3 Tax-on-$x$-shortfall} (Pigou-mirror): tax based on private consumption above a baseline $\bar x^i$, i.e.\ $t(x^i - \bar x^i)$. Equivalent to D.1 with a baseline shift.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Subsidy vs tax: same margin, opposite wealth effect.}}

\begin{tabular}{p{10cm}|p{13cm}|p{15cm}}
\textbf{Instrument} & \textbf{Per-unit incentive on $y^i$} & \textbf{Lump-sum component (wealth effect)} \\
\hline
C.\ Bilateral subsidy $s y^i$ & $-s$ on price of $y$ & $+0$ --- agent richer overall \\
\hline
D.1 Tax on $x$ & $+t$ on price of $x$ (equivalent to $-t$ on $y/x$ ratio) & $-t x^i$ --- agent poorer \\
\hline
D.2 Shortfall tax $t(y^* - y^i)$ & $-t$ on price of $y$ & $-t y^*$ --- agent poorer \\
\hline
D.3 $x$-overconsumption tax $t(x^i - \bar x^i)$ & same as D.1 with baseline shift & lump-sum depends on baseline \\
\end{tabular}

\textcolor{purple}{\emph{All four (C + D.1 + D.2 + D.3) achieve the same Samuelson allocation.} They differ in wealth effects: subsidies make agents richer, taxes poorer. Choice is purely distributional / political.}

\textcolor{purple}{\emph{The wrong direction (don't do this):} \textbf{taxing $y^i$ directly} (or subsidising $x^i$) makes things worse --- $y$ is under-provided in CE, so taxing $y$ widens the gap. The taxes that work in the public-good case all increase the effective relative price of $x$ (or decrease the effective relative price of $y$), never the reverse.}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Comparison.}}

\begin{tabular}{p{8cm}|p{15cm}|p{15cm}}
& \textbf{Information required} & \textbf{Decentralisation?} \\
\hline
A.\ Quota & $y^*$ + distribution of $\bar y^i$ & No (mandate) \\
\hline
B.\ Public provision + lump-sum tax & $y^*$ + each $T^i$ (Lindahl for Pareto-improvement) & No (state produces) \\
\hline
C.\ Bilateral subsidy & each $MRS^j$ at $\hat y$ & \textbf{Yes} --- prices implement the planner \\
\end{tabular}

\textcolor{purple}{\emph{Slogan:}} A and B require the planner to compute the Samuelson level $y^*$ and impose it. \textbf{C is the price-based decentralisation}: each agent is subsidised by the positive externality he creates, so his private FOC becomes the social FOC. \textbf{Sum of MRSs naturally equals MRT at the equilibrium.}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Cross-refs:}} CE for public goods (under-provision gap), planner + Samuelson, Lindahl pricing, externality solutions (Pigou tax for negative externalities --- this is its mirror image for positive).

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Public goods --- Lindahl pricing equilibrium.}}

\emph{Setting: $U^i(x^i, y)$, both agents consume the SAME $y$ (non-rivalry), production $z = c(y)$, $c'>0$ convex. Standard textbook treatment (e.g.\ MWG §16.G, Varian Ch.\ 24).}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Idea.}} Replace the single market price $p_y$ with \textbf{personalised prices} $\tau^i$ for each agent. KEY: THE FIRM IS PRODUCING just 1y, consumed by all agents (all agents consuem the same quanity**), but they pay it individually atht the price they want, they receive the full y in return. 

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Agent $i$'s problem (in dollar-terms).}}
\[ \max_{x^i,\, y}\;\; U^i(x^i, y) \;\;\text{sub.}\;\; p_x\, x^i + \tau^i\, y \;=\; p_x\, \omega^i + \theta^i\, \pi^*. \]
\emph{Each agent picks his preferred $y$ at his personalised price $\tau^i$. Since $y$ is the common public-good level, all agents' demands for $y$ must agree in equilibrium.}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{FOCs.}}
\[ \partial \mathcal{L}^i/\partial x^i:\;\; \dfrac{\partial U^i}{\partial x^i} = \lambda^i\, p_x \;\Rightarrow\; \lambda^i = \dfrac{1}{p_x}\dfrac{\partial U^i}{\partial x^i}, \]
\[ \partial \mathcal{L}^i/\partial y:\;\; \dfrac{\partial U^i}{\partial y} = \lambda^i\, \tau^i \;\Rightarrow\; \boxed{\;\; MRS^i_{y,x} = \dfrac{\tau^i}{p_x} \;\;} \quad\text{(each agent's MRS = his personalised price ratio).} \]

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Firm side --- revenue from personalised prices.}} Each unit of $y$ produced is sold to EACH agent at his personalised price $\tau^i$. Total revenue from one unit of $y$ is $\sum_i \tau^i = \tau^1 + \tau^2$. The firm maximises
\[ \pi(y) \;=\; \bigl(\tau^1 + \tau^2\bigr) y \;-\; p_x\, c(y). \]
\textbf{Firm FOC:}
\[ \boxed{\;\; \tau^1 + \tau^2   = p_x c'(y)} \]

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Lindahl implements Samuelson} --- substitute agent FOCs into firm FOC.} From each agent's FOC: $\tau^i = p_x \cdot MRS^i|_{y^*}$. Sub into the firm FOC:
\[ p_x \cdot MRS^1\Big|_{y^*} \;+\; p_x \cdot MRS^2\Big|_{y^*} \;=\; p_x\, c'(y^*) \;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\; MRS^1 + MRS^2 = c'(y^*). \]
\[ \boxed{\;\; \sum_i MRS^i\Big|_{y^*} \;=\; c'(y^*) \;=\; MRT \;\; \text{(Samuelson condition).}\;\;} \]

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Personalised prices = personal willingness to pay.}} From the agent FOC, $\tau^i = p_x \cdot MRS^i|_{y^*}$. Each agent pays a price equal to his marginal willingness to pay: high-MRS agents pay more, low-MRS agents pay less. \textbf{No free-riding because the price extracts his true MRS.}
